"""Original fictional case-information practice, not statutory casework training."""
def build(c, m):
    return c(67, 'Child Protection Case Management and Information Practice', 'Child Protection\nCase Management\nand Information Practice',
        'Review fictional case-information and referral records for accuracy, necessary sharing, continuity and qualified responsibility without handling real child case files.', [
        m('Purpose roles and case-information boundaries', 'Distinguish introductory case-process reasoning from authorised child-protection case management.', [
            'Child-protection case management concerns coordinated support for an individual child and family through an authorised process. It is more than maintaining a spreadsheet or forwarding a message. The CPMS summary describes individualised, coordinated support involving direct help and links to services. In this course, learners examine invented process records rather than conduct a real assessment or manage a caseload. Start by asking what the proposed record is for, who is responsible for the case and which decisions require professional or statutory authority in the actual setting. [S42]',
            'An appropriate information record supports a defined action. A referral outline might identify the type of support sought, the responsible role, the status of a response and a review point. It should not become an unrestricted narrative about a child’s life. The CPMS information-management guidance connects necessary collection and sharing to contextualised safeguards. Distinguish a learning worksheet from an operational system: the worksheet contains only supplied fictional facts, while real systems need approved governance, access controls, retention arrangements and competent staff. A useful-looking template does not establish those conditions. [S42]',
            'The Alliance published a second edition of its inter-agency case-management guidelines in December 2024. This identifies an updated resource for qualified teams to consult alongside local procedures. The present appraisal accessed the official resource page and selected indexed publisher excerpts, not the full operational manual. Consequently, this course does not present a copied sequence, global form or universal deadline as an academy protocol. A learner should identify the need for current authoritative guidance and supervision rather than infer that a short course replaces those resources. [S43]',
            'Clarity about roles prevents false completion claims. Receiving a concern, reviewing it, accepting a referral, providing a service and authorising closure are different events. An organisation should identify who can do each task and how unresolved responsibilities are escalated through suitable arrangements. In the invented case, record what is known and what remains unconfirmed. Do not appoint yourself as a statutory decision-maker, investigate an allegation or promise that another agency has accepted responsibility merely because an email was sent. The learning outcome is a bounded analysis of information and coordination. [S41, S42]'],
            'A fictional team calls a shared spreadsheet a complete case-management service. A learner is assigned authority to investigate, approve a placement and close referrals after sending emails.',
            'Draft a role-and-purpose note separating administrative information tasks, qualified case responsibilities, unconfirmed service arrangements and activities outside the learner’s authority.',
            'A spreadsheet is a support tool, not proof of a complete service. Separate recording from assessment, statutory decisions, referral acceptance and closure; retain qualified responsibility and uncertainty.', ['S42', 'S43', 'S41'], [
                ('A record system is not a complete case-management service.', 'The fictional team has a spreadsheet but no caseworker or service arrangements. What is justified?', 'It has an information tool; responsibility and support arrangements still need to be established.', ['It already provides complete case management.', 'The spreadsheet author can approve placements.', 'Every listed case can be marked safely closed.'], 'An information tool cannot substitute for authorised, coordinated support.'),
                ('Information tasks need a defined purpose.', 'An invented referral form collects extensive family details without stating how they will be used. What should be clarified first?', 'The legitimate action, responsible role and necessary information for that action.', ['How to collect additional details before any purpose is set.', 'Which classmates should receive the full narrative.', 'How to imply that a form is an official statutory assessment.'], 'Purpose and responsibility guide proportionate information handling.'),
                ('Updated guidance must not be replaced by an invented protocol.', 'A learner has read only the landing page for the 2024 case-management update. What can they accurately say?', 'An updated resource exists; operational application requires the actual guidance and suitable local supervision.', ['They have appraised every form in the full manual.', 'The landing page establishes a universal reporting deadline.', 'The course grants authority to use any global case form independently.'], 'A resource identification is distinct from whole-manual appraisal or implementation authority.'),
                ('Sending a referral does not establish acceptance.', 'A fictional email requests help from a service but receives no reply. Which status is accurate?', 'Sent; acceptance and follow-up responsibility remain unconfirmed.', ['Service completed.', 'Referral accepted and case closed.', 'The child’s needs have been met.'], 'Status should reflect actual evidence of the receiving service’s response.'),
                ('A learner cannot create statutory authority by recording it.', 'An invented worksheet assigns the learner power to approve a child’s placement. What should be corrected?', 'Identify the relevant authorised decision role and retain the learner’s introductory analytical boundary.', ['Keep the assignment because it appears on a form.', 'Ask classmates to vote on a real placement.', 'Treat a course badge as a statutory appointment.'], 'A classroom record does not confer professional or statutory decision authority.')]),
        m('Accurate records necessary sharing and confidentiality', 'Analyse fictional records for attribution, relevance, access and information-sharing dependencies.', [
            'Accurate records distinguish observation, reported information, interpretation and an agreed action. If an invented adult reports that a child was absent, that is not automatically proof of why the child was absent. Record the source and uncertainty without adding an unsupported conclusion. A short factual note can be more useful than a detailed but speculative account. Information-management principles in the CPMS support necessary and appropriately protected records; they do not authorise learners to gather evidence from real children or turn their coursework into an operational case file. [S42]',
            'Necessary sharing asks what a particular recipient needs for an authorised purpose, rather than whether an entire file can be forwarded. Consider the recipient’s role, the relevant basis for sharing, the child’s appropriate involvement and the approved safeguards for the actual setting. A label such as confidential does not establish secure handling. Do not promise absolute confidentiality or invent a legal exception. When a real situation is uncertain, a designated qualified role must apply the applicable procedures. The fictional exercise should identify that dependency instead of resolving a real disclosure decision. [S42, S41]',
            'Access arrangements need attention to ordinary practices as well as technology. An unrestricted group chat, shared device or personal inbox may expose information beyond those who need it. Removing a name may not be enough where a small group can recognise a child from contextual details. For coursework, use only invented identifiers and circumstances. In an actual service, information storage, retention, access review and safe disposal would need appropriate organisational and legal governance. This course does not prescribe a particular product, retention period or security architecture as universally compliant. [S41, S42]',
            'Children’s participation in information use should be understandable and proportionate. Explain the purpose and limits of the process through suitable communication support, without demanding that a child produce a personal story to participate. The participation review highlights how context and relationships shape whether a child is heard. In the invented record, separate a child’s stated view from an adult’s summary and provide a suitable way to question an inaccurate account. An accurate record may still be incomplete; acknowledging that limit is preferable to filling gaps with assumptions or fabricated consent. [R51, S42]'],
            'A fictional note turns an unverified report into a finding of neglect. The full narrative is copied to an open group chat, with a child’s name removed but recognisable details retained.',
            'Rewrite the note using invented information: distinguish report from inference, state the necessary sharing purpose, identify a qualified decision owner and describe a proportionate access correction.',
            'Retain attribution and uncertainty, share only through authorised arrangements and avoid assuming that removing a name makes information anonymous. Do not invent consent or legal findings.', ['S42', 'S41', 'R51'], [
                ('Reported information must not become an unsupported finding.', 'An invented adult reports a child’s absence but gives no explanation. Which note is defensible?', 'Record the attributed report and the unknown reason, without declaring a finding of neglect.', ['State that neglect was confirmed.', 'Invent an explanation that makes the file complete.', 'Remove the source so the note appears independently verified.'], 'Attribution and uncertainty distinguish a report from a professional finding.'),
                ('Necessary sharing is purpose-specific.', 'A fictional recipient needs an appointment detail, but a worker proposes sending the entire case narrative. What should be reviewed?', 'What that authorised recipient needs for the defined task and the relevant sharing safeguards.', ['Whether more information is always better.', 'Whether a group chat can replace approved governance.', 'Whether all case details should become training examples.'], 'A particular action does not automatically justify unrestricted sharing.'),
                ('Removing a name may not prevent recognition.', 'An invented village summary removes a child’s name but retains a unique family description. What risk remains?', 'People may identify the child from the contextual details.', ['The information is automatically anonymous.', 'A name is the only possible identifier.', 'A confidentiality label prevents all disclosure.'], 'Indirect identification can remain possible, especially in small groups.'),
                ('A confidentiality label is not an access control.', 'A fictional case note is marked confidential and placed in an unrestricted folder. What is missing?', 'An approved access arrangement suited to the purpose and sensitivity of the information.', ['A larger confidential stamp.', 'An assurance that no review is needed.', 'A statement that the course has certified the folder.'], 'Actual handling arrangements matter beyond labels.'),
                ('Unknown consent or sharing authority must remain unknown.', 'A fictional record has no evidence that the relevant sharing arrangements were explained. What should a learner do?', 'Record the gap and refer the actual decision to the designated qualified role and local procedure.', ['Insert a fabricated consent statement.', 'Treat silence as proof of informed agreement.', 'Declare a universal legal exemption without checking the setting.'], 'The worksheet must not manufacture consent or legal authority.')]),
        m('Referral continuity review and responsible closure', 'Produce an original fictional follow-up record that distinguishes referral, service delivery and closure decisions.', [
            'Continuity depends on knowing who is responsible for the next action. A referral record should distinguish the request, the receiving service’s response, any agreed arrangement and the next review point. When acceptance remains unconfirmed, keep that uncertainty visible and identify a suitable escalation route. The CPMS case-management summary describes coordinated support rather than isolated transactions. A completed email or form is therefore an activity record, not evidence that support reached the child or that the relevant concern has been resolved. [S42]',
            'Service availability can change because of capacity, access, displacement or other local conditions. Do not promise a service merely because it appears in a directory. An invented plan can state that availability needs confirmation, describe a conditional alternative and name the responsible qualified role. Organisational safeguarding guidance supports clear local procedures and responsibilities. This course does not authorise the learner to improvise emergency arrangements, transfer a child, mediate allegations or make a statutory decision when a service is unavailable. Recognising a boundary and unresolved dependency is an important analytical skill. [S41, S42]',
            'Review should consider whether the plan still fits the stated purpose and whether the child and family have an appropriate opportunity to be heard. Their views may reveal a practical barrier that an administrative completion count misses. Record the actual status and the reasons for a proposed change through the authorised process. The participation evidence supports attention to relationships and context; it does not establish that collecting more feedback always improves safety. Use a fictional scenario to practise a proportionate review without creating a real child-contact exercise or collecting personal case material. [R51, S42]',
            'Closure is a decision under applicable professional and organisational arrangements, not a way to tidy a spreadsheet. A learner may identify that evidence, consultation or follow-up is missing without determining a real case outcome. Keep a traceable history of invented status changes, reasons, responsible roles and review points. If an earlier entry was inaccurate, correct it in a way that preserves the history rather than pretending that the error never occurred. The final worksheet demonstrates introductory reasoning about continuity and accountability; it is not completed placement, verified professional practice or a substitute for the school’s separate assessment requirements. [S41, S42]'],
            'An invented service has not accepted a referral, transport remains uncertain and the child’s stated access concern is omitted. The team closes the spreadsheet entry to meet a reporting target.',
            'Draft a follow-up record with the actual referral status, child-view gap, responsible qualified role, conditional next action and reason that closure cannot be assumed from the reporting target.',
            'Keep acceptance and access uncertainties visible. Assign a responsible review route and distinguish administrative completion from service delivery and a properly authorised closure decision.', ['S42', 'S41', 'R51'], [
                ('A referral request is not evidence that support arrived.', 'A fictional report counts every referral email as a completed service. What should be distinguished?', 'Requests sent, responses received, agreed arrangements and evidence of actual support.', ['Only the number of emails sent.', 'A reporting target and a verified protection outcome as the same thing.', 'Unconfirmed services as automatically delivered.'], 'Continuity requires an evidence-based account of the different stages.'),
                ('A service directory does not guarantee current availability.', 'A fictional directory lists a service whose capacity has not been checked. What should the plan state?', 'Availability is unconfirmed, with a responsible role and conditional next step.', ['A guaranteed appointment and outcome.', 'That no qualified follow-up is needed.', 'That the learner can independently transfer a child to the service.'], 'An entry in a directory is not confirmation of capacity or authority.'),
                ('Review should consider the child’s relevant access concern.', 'An invented child’s transport concern is absent from a follow-up summary. What is appropriate?', 'Record the omitted concern and seek its consideration through the authorised review process.', ['Declare that all access barriers are resolved.', 'Replace the concern with an adult’s assumption.', 'Publish a real family’s travel details as a teaching example.'], 'A review can identify practical gaps without exposing real information.'),
                ('A reporting target does not authorise closure.', 'An invented team marks an unresolved referral closed to improve its monthly statistics. What is the strongest critique?', 'Closure must follow the applicable authorised decision process and actual evidence, not a numerical target.', ['Any target permits changing case status.', 'A blank spreadsheet is proof of improved child safety.', 'A course learner may close every case independently.'], 'Administrative convenience is not a defensible case decision.'),
                ('Corrections should preserve an accountable history.', 'A fictional referral was mistakenly recorded as accepted. How should the invented record be corrected?', 'Retain a traceable correction with the actual status, reason and responsible review role.', ['Erase every earlier entry without explanation.', 'Keep the false status to avoid a lower completion count.', 'Add a fabricated provider signature.'], 'Traceable correction supports accountability without inventing evidence.')])
    ])
